-- title:
--   Admissible cutoffs form a closed set
-- statement:
--   For $w:X\to\mathbb{R}$, $A\subseteq X$ and $d,u\in\mathbb{R}$, the truncated excess is $E_A(d,u)=\sum_x\min(w(x),u)([x\in A]-(1-d))$. A cutoff $u$ is admissible for $(w,A,m,d)$ if $0\le u\le1$, every $x$ with $w(x)>u$ lies in $A$, and $mu\le E_A(d,u)$. The heavy set at $u$ is $\{x:w(x)>u\}$. For fixed $w,A,m,d$, the set
--   $$
--   \{u\in\mathbb{R}:\ u\text{ is admissible for }(w,A,m,d)\}
--   $$
--   is closed.
--
--   Since it is also bounded, a maximal admissible cutoff exists whenever some cutoff is admissible.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ExpectationThreshold/SelectorTruncationCompact.lean#L33-L56

